# See .github/workflows/build.yml's Trivy scan job (trivyignores input).
#
# Every entry here is investigated and justified individually -- this file
# is for genuinely unreachable findings only, never a blanket suppression.
# Re-check each entry whenever the base image or requirements.txt changes;
# don't just keep adding to it.

# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 -- msgpack 1.1.2, HIGH.
# This is pip's own vendored copy (site-packages/pip/_vendor/msgpack),
# bundled inside the python:3.12-slim base image's pip installation for
# pip's *own* internal HTTP-cache serialization. This app's code never
# imports msgpack, and pip itself is only invoked at build time (`pip
# install -r requirements.txt` in the Dockerfile) -- it is not present in
# the running process, never receives network/user input at runtime, and
# ships in the image purely as a byproduct of how pip is packaged.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# CVE-2025-47273 -- setuptools 70.3.0, HIGH.
# Trivy is parsing pip/_vendor/vendor.txt, a plain-text manifest pip ships
# documenting which versions it vendors -- it is not an installed package
# (confirmed: no setuptools files exist anywhere in the image, `python3 -c
# "import setuptools"` fails with ModuleNotFoundError). False positive from
# text-file parsing, not a real installed dependency.
CVE-2025-47273
